1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
Welcome to this new in-depth analysis.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:08,880
Today's title poses a very strong question: does losing your job push you to crime?

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:12,679
One might wonder if there's truly a direct and tangible link.

4
00:00:12,679 --> 00:00:18,559
Well, today we won't make assumptions, but we'll explore the answers contained in a fascinating

5
00:00:18,559 --> 00:00:23,320
2022 study, published in the renowned Econometrica journal.

6
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:28,560
We will analyze a huge amount of real data to uncover the complex dynamics between employment

7
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:33,800
and human behavior, focusing on a vast case study: Brazil.

8
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:39,000
No unfounded theories, just the findings of facts. Let's start right away.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,640
Okay, let's dive right in and take a look at this graph.

10
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:48,960
It shows us the general overview of Brazil from 1991 to 2017, and a very clear, strong

11
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:52,680
macroeconomic correlation immediately stands out.

12
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:57,280
Looking at the orange line for employment, you'll notice that every time it drops, the green line,

13
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:01,200
which represents the homicide rate, takes a leap upwards.

14
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:06,400
To understand better, during this period, a 16% drop in employment corresponded to an impressive

15
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:10,799
62% increase in homicides.

16
00:01:10,799 --> 00:01:15,640
It's an astounding starting point, but as we know well, correlation doesn't necessarily mean

17
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:19,359
causation. We need to dig much deeper.

18
00:01:19,359 --> 00:01:24,079
Exactly. To distinguish a mere statistical coincidence from a genuine

19
00:01:24,079 --> 00:01:28,560
cause-and-effect relationship, researchers had a brilliant idea: they decided

20
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:32,560
to isolate only mass layoffs, because those are precisely what's needed.

21
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:37,799
In economics, a mass layoff represents what is defined as an exogenous shock.

22
00:01:37,799 --> 00:01:41,840
Imagine a company that suddenly cuts more than a third of its workforce.

23
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:45,519
We can be virtually certain that those jobs were lost due to a market fluctuation

24
00:01:45,519 --> 00:01:49,879
or a corporate crisis, not because the individual employee started

25
00:01:49,879 --> 00:01:51,480
behaving badly, right?

26
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:54,680
This way, we eliminate the root cause, the doubt that the layoff was caused

27
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:58,880
by a person's pre-existing criminal inclination, and we can surgically isolate

28
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:00,760
the impact of unemployment.

29
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:06,600
Prepare yourselves, because the results of this statistical isolation are nothing short of shocking.

30
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:11,840
The data tells us that the probability of committing a crime increases by an average of 23%

31
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:14,319
for workers who experience a layoff.

32
00:02:14,319 --> 00:02:19,159
A 23% jump – we're talking about an increase of almost a quarter compared to typical behavior,

33
00:02:19,159 --> 00:02:22,599
recorded in the year following job loss.

34
00:02:22,599 --> 00:02:26,840
And what's even more impressive is that this rate remains high and stable for up to

35
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:31,159
4 years after the layoff. This isn't a momentary outburst;

36
00:02:31,159 --> 00:02:35,240
it's a prolonged and deeply rooted change in the behavior of those who suddenly find themselves

37
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:36,240
unemployed.

38
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:41,240
Now, the truly interesting thing about this slide is understanding what types of crimes

39
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:46,120
we are talking about. By breaking down the total effect, we see that not all crimes increase

40
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:47,120
in the same way.

41
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:51,560
As was perhaps predictable, crimes with a very strong economic motivation, such as

42
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:57,800
thefts, robberies, or drug trafficking, skyrocket with an incredible 43% increase, with

43
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:03,560
robberies alone showing a staggering 91% increase. But be careful, because it's not just a matter

44
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:09,240
of desperately seeking money. Violent crimes also see a 17% rise,

45
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:15,400
including homicides, which show a disturbing 32% increase. This broad scope of effects

46
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:18,439
forces us to look beyond simple economic necessity.

47
00:03:18,439 --> 00:03:24,120
In fact, there's a crucial detail that emerges from the data: a documented

48
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:28,960
generalized increase even in all those offenses that have literally zero economic motivation.

49
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:34,520
We're talking about vandalism, damage to private property, serious traffic violations,

50
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:38,960
public brawls, offenses, and defamation.

51
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:44,120
Seeing these specific categories soar gives us a very first and crucial clue:

52
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:48,960
enormous psychological stress and emotional trauma play a crucial role, operating

53
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:51,200
in parallel with the loss of income.

54
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:56,200
The human mind, subjected to such fierce emotional pressure, reacts in disparate and

55
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,240
self-destructive ways for society.

56
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:04,000
And, unfortunately, the consequences of this pressure don't stop with the individual directly affected; there's

57
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
a tragic spillover effect, a true domino effect on the family unit.

58
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,879
The numbers reveal that the probability of a criminal charge increases by a worrying

59
00:04:13,879 --> 00:04:20,959
18% even for the cohabiting children of laid-off workers. 18% – it's as if the tension,

60
00:04:20,959 --> 00:04:26,839
financial difficulties, and fear experienced by the parent infiltrate the home,

61
00:04:26,839 --> 00:04:32,480
eroding the environment to the point of pushing even younger generations towards illegality.

62
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:37,399
At this point, utilizing highly advanced algorithms, the study succeeded in constructing

63
00:04:37,399 --> 00:04:41,480
a very precise "identikit" of individuals most at risk.

64
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:46,400
The researchers followed through the data, showing us that the steepest slopes, and therefore

65
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:52,000
the absolute highest risks, are concentrated around two core variables: age and job tenure.

66
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,400
This allows us to zoom in and see who experiences the most devastating impact

67
00:04:57,400 --> 00:05:00,560
of the trauma, beyond simple general averages.

68
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:05,120
In short, young workers with only a few months of tenure

69
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:10,040
in the company are statistically in the hardest-hit group. But do you know what the

70
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:15,200
truly surprising part is? This peak occurs completely independently of the basic education level

71
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:19,760
or the wealth of the area where one lives. In a vast and heterogeneous country

72
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:24,080
like Brazil, it doesn't make much difference if the local crime rate

73
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:28,680
is low or if one has a good education. The explosive combination always remains that between

74
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:32,280
being young and having just started a job.

75
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:37,879
Excellent, but having reached this stage of the analysis, we have a good old mystery to solve.

76
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:43,639
What is the original spark? Why does all this happen? Does the increase in crime occur

77
00:05:43,639 --> 00:05:48,959
simply because, finding themselves unemployed, people suddenly have empty days

78
00:05:48,959 --> 00:05:54,279
and too much free time to get into trouble? Or is the reason brutally material,

79
00:05:54,279 --> 00:05:58,800
meaning that people are willing to do anything because they don't have the necessary cash

80
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:03,079
to make ends meet? And this magnificently illustrates the great

81
00:06:03,079 --> 00:06:08,240
theoretical debate that has fascinated scholars for decades, contrasting two clear views.

82
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:14,120
On one hand, there's the free-time abundance hypothesis, also known as the incapacitation effect.

83
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:19,439
Simply put, clocking in for 8 hours keeps you occupied and out of trouble.

84
00:06:19,439 --> 00:06:24,599
On the other hand, we find the liquidity constraint hypothesis, that is, the pure and raw

85
00:06:24,599 --> 00:06:28,959
desperation caused by not having enough cash to meet basic needs.

86
00:06:29,640 --> 00:06:34,280
Which of these two powerful forces truly pushes an individual beyond the line of legality?

87
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:39,120
To discover this unequivocally, researchers unearthed a natural experiment that was literally

88
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:44,240
perfect. In Brazil, there is an extremely rigid institutional rule.

89
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:49,320
To be eligible for unemployment benefits, exactly 16 months must have passed since your

90
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:55,360
previous layoff. In short, if you lose your job at 15 months, you don't receive a penny.

91
00:06:55,480 --> 00:07:00,319
Whereas someone who loses it just as the 16th month strikes can receive up to 5 months of assured

92
00:07:00,319 --> 00:07:05,720
salary. This pinpoint temporal threshold gives us the rare opportunity to compare

93
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:11,040
two statistically identical groups of people, differing by only one vital

94
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:13,879
element: immediate access to cash.

95
00:07:13,879 --> 00:07:18,120
Drumroll, please? The data speaks volumes.

96
00:07:18,120 --> 00:07:23,759
Fortunate workers who are eligible to receive benefits show a drastic

97
00:07:23,759 --> 00:07:30,199
reduction in crime rates in the first 6 months. A 21% drop compared

98
00:07:30,199 --> 00:07:35,759
to those who missed the requirements by just a few days. This number is a milestone.

99
00:07:35,759 --> 00:07:42,560
21% means that providing a safety net, a bit of financial breathing room, instantly extinguishes

100
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:47,279
a huge portion of the crime triggered by job loss.

101
00:07:47,279 --> 00:07:53,279
So the crucial point is encapsulated precisely in this formidable deduction from the study.

102
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:59,520
Eligibility for unemployment benefits almost completely offsets the potential increase

103
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:05,120
in crime. And there you have it, our mystery solved, case closed. The data proves that

104
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:09,640
subsidized workers even take longer to find new employment, and therefore,

105
00:08:09,640 --> 00:08:14,440
paradoxically, they enjoy a flood of extra free time. Yet, they commit many

106
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:19,600
fewer crimes. This completely dismantles the idea that an excess of idleness generates illegality.

107
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:25,360
The real culprit, without a shadow of a doubt, is the sudden absence of money.

108
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:29,840
As we approach the conclusion of our investigation, we can finally point the finger at

109
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:35,519
the real culprits. The true drivers of post-layoff crime are two. The first is

110
00:08:35,519 --> 00:08:39,800
the severe and immediate liquidity constraint, that agonizing inability to afford

111
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:45,120
the bare minimum to live. The second, acting simultaneously, is

112
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:50,200
the devastating impact of psychological stress and a sense of failure. It's certainly not

113
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:55,399
too much free time that causes damage, but an explosive cocktail of economic desperation combined

114
00:08:55,399 --> 00:09:00,879
with a collapse of personal identity. But there's one last provocative thought with which

115
00:09:00,879 --> 00:09:05,720
we must conclude this analysis. What happens to the human mind and the fabric of our

116
00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:11,879
cities when this vital support suddenly stops? The researchers point out

117
00:09:11,879 --> 00:09:16,799
an objective reality that sends shivers down one's spine: the protective effect of benefits vanishes

118
00:09:16,799 --> 00:09:21,759
at the exact moment payments stop. Crime violently rises again

119
00:09:21,759 --> 00:09:26,600
precisely in that fateful month when the taps of assistance are turned off. It's a fact that

120
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:30,759
forces us to deeply reflect on the extreme fragility of our social safety nets,

121
00:09:30,759 --> 00:09:35,799
because when a society stops supporting those who fall into a sudden abyss,

122
00:09:35,799 --> 00:09:41,039
the repercussions inevitably end up affecting everyone. Until the next analysis.